### What are the three pieces of exactly-once?

Exactly-once semantics means each input record affects the output and the offsets exactly once, with no duplicates and no loss. It is built from three pieces.

```java
package com.bank.eos;

public class ExactlyOncePieces {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Piece 1: Idempotent producer");
        System.out.println("  PID + sequence -> broker drops retry duplicates");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Piece 2: Kafka transactions");
        System.out.println("  Atomic commit of output + offsets");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Piece 3: Epoch fencing");
        System.out.println("  Zombie producers rejected via epoch bump");
    }
}
```

```text
Piece 1: Idempotent producer
  PID + sequence -> broker drops retry duplicates

Piece 2: Kafka transactions
  Atomic commit of output + offsets

Piece 3: Epoch fencing
  Zombie producers rejected via epoch bump
```

### How does the idempotent producer work?

When idempotence is enabled, the broker assigns the producer a unique PID. The producer tags each record with a per-partition sequence number. If a retry sends the same sequence, the broker drops the duplicate.

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class IdempotentSetup {
    public static Properties config() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "broker1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, "true");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.MAX_IN_FLIGHT_REQUESTS_PER_CONNECTION, 5);
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Idempotent producer:");
        System.out.println("  Broker assigns PID=100");
        System.out.println("  Record sent: PID=100, seq=5 -> written");
        System.out.println("  ACK lost, retry: PID=100, seq=5 -> DUPLICATE, ack only");
    }
}
```

```text
Idempotent producer:
  Broker assigns PID=100
  Record sent: PID=100, seq=5 -> written
  ACK lost, retry: PID=100, seq=5 -> DUPLICATE, ack only
```

### How do transactions work?

Transactions extend idempotence across multiple partitions and tie the produce and the offset commit together.

```java
package com.bank.eos;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecords;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.OffsetAndMetadata;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.KafkaProducer;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.common.TopicPartition;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Properties;

public class ExactlyOnceTransactionLoop {
    public static void run(KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer,
                            KafkaProducer<String, String> producer) {
        producer.initTransactions();

        while (true) {
            ConsumerRecords<String, String> records =
                consumer.poll(Duration.ofMillis(100));
            if (records.isEmpty()) continue;

            producer.beginTransaction();
            try {
                records.forEach(record -> {
                    // Process: debit + credit
                    producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("settlements",
                        record.key(), "{\"settled\":true}"));
                });

                // Commit offsets INSIDE the transaction
                Map<TopicPartition, OffsetAndMetadata> offsets = new HashMap<>();
                records.partitions().forEach(tp -> {
                    long last = records.records(tp)
                        .get(records.records(tp).size() - 1).offset();
                    offsets.put(tp, new OffsetAndMetadata(last + 1));
                });
                producer.sendOffsetsToTransaction(offsets, consumer.groupMetadata());
                producer.commitTransaction();
            } catch (Exception e) {
                producer.abortTransaction();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### How does the transaction coordinator work?

The transaction coordinator is a broker-side component that manages each transactional.id. It keeps state in the internal `__transaction_state` topic. On commit, it writes commit markers to every partition the transaction touched.

```java
package com.bank.eos;

public class TransactionCoordinatorSteps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Transaction coordinator flow:");
        System.out.println("  1. Producer calls initTransactions()");
        System.out.println("     Coordinator assigns/bumps epoch for tx.id");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  2. Producer calls beginTransaction()");
        System.out.println("     Coordinator logs: tx OPEN");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  3. Producer sends records to partitions");
        System.out.println("     Coordinator tracks which partitions are in tx");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  4. Producer calls commitTransaction()");
        System.out.println("     Coordinator logs: tx PREPARE_COMMIT");
        System.out.println("     Coordinator writes COMMIT markers to all partitions");
        System.out.println("     Coordinator logs: tx COMMITTED");
    }
}
```

```text
Transaction coordinator flow:
  1. Producer calls initTransactions()
     Coordinator assigns/bumps epoch for tx.id

  2. Producer calls beginTransaction()
     Coordinator logs: tx OPEN

  3. Producer sends records to partitions
     Coordinator tracks which partitions are in tx

  4. Producer calls commitTransaction()
     Coordinator logs: tx PREPARE_COMMIT
     Coordinator writes COMMIT markers to all partitions
     Coordinator logs: tx COMMITTED
```

### What are commit markers?

Commit markers are special control records written to every partition the transaction touched, including the consumer offsets topic. They tell consumers that the records before them in this transaction are now committed.

```java
package com.bank.eos;

public class CommitMarkers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Commit markers written to:");
        System.out.println("  settlements-0  : COMMIT marker");
        System.out.println("  settlements-3  : COMMIT marker");
        System.out.println("  __consumer_offsets-12 : COMMIT marker");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Consumer with read_committed:");
        System.out.println("  Sees records only after COMMIT marker");
        System.out.println("  Skips records from ABORTED transactions");
    }
}
```

```text
Commit markers written to:
  settlements-0  : COMMIT marker
  settlements-3  : COMMIT marker
  __consumer_offsets-12 : COMMIT marker

Consumer with read_committed:
  Sees records only after COMMIT marker
  Skips records from ABORTED transactions
```

### How does epoch fencing stop zombies?

When a producer calls `initTransactions()`, the coordinator bumps the epoch for that transactional.id. If an old instance tries to write or commit with the old epoch, it is rejected with a ProducerFencedException.

```java
package com.bank.eos;

public class EpochFencing {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Epoch fencing:");
        System.out.println("  Instance A starts: tx.id=settlement-tx-1, epoch=1");
        System.out.println("  Instance A hangs (zombie)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Instance B starts: tx.id=settlement-tx-1, epoch=2");
        System.out.println("  Coordinator bumps epoch from 1 to 2");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Instance A wakes up, tries to commit with epoch=1");
        System.out.println("  Coordinator: REJECTED -- ProducerFencedException");
        System.out.println("  Only Instance B (epoch=2) can write");
    }
}
```

```text
Epoch fencing:
  Instance A starts: tx.id=settlement-tx-1, epoch=1
  Instance A hangs (zombie)

  Instance B starts: tx.id=settlement-tx-1, epoch=2
  Coordinator bumps epoch from 1 to 2

  Instance A wakes up, tries to commit with epoch=1
  Coordinator: REJECTED -- ProducerFencedException
  Only Instance B (epoch=2) can write
```

### How does the consumer participate?

Consumers must set `isolation.level=read_committed` to see only committed transaction records.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class ReadCommittedConfig {
    public static Properties config() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "broker1:9092");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "ledger-service");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.ISOLATION_LEVEL_CONFIG, "read_committed");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, "false");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("read_committed: sees only committed records");
        System.out.println("read_uncommitted: sees everything (including aborted)");
    }
}
```

```text
read_committed: sees only committed records
read_uncommitted: sees everything (including aborted)
```

### What are the limitations?

Exactly-once is end-to-end only within Kafka. The moment you write to an external system (database), you need that system to be idempotent or use an outbox pattern. Transactions add latency and coordinator overhead.

```java
package com.bank.limitation;

public class ExactlyOnceLimitations {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Limitations:");
        System.out.println("  1. Only within Kafka -- cannot roll back PostgreSQL");
        System.out.println("  2. Transactions add latency (coordinator overhead)");
        System.out.println("  3. Use only where correctness demands it (money movement)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("For external systems:");
        System.out.println("  - Make DB writes idempotent (upsert on trade id)");
        System.out.println("  - Or use the outbox pattern");
    }
}
```

```text
Limitations:
  1. Only within Kafka -- cannot roll back PostgreSQL
  2. Transactions add latency (coordinator overhead)
  3. Use only where correctness demands it (money movement)

For external systems:
  - Make DB writes idempotent (upsert on trade id)
  - Or use the outbox pattern
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The consume-process-produce loop with `sendOffsetsToTransaction` is the foundation of exactly-once stream processing. The transaction coordinator uses a two-phase commit internally. Transactions add real cost, so reserve them for hard atomicity requirements. External side effects are outside the transaction boundary.
